Title:
PLATINUMPICK — Windows Kernel-Mode Shellcode Development Framework (WKMSDF)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
PLATINUMPICK is a C++20 framework for authoring Windows x64 kernel-mode position-independent code (PIC) / shellcode. It provides conventions and helper macros to avoid common PIC pitfalls (no globals, stack-based strings) and to resolve kernel exports at runtime via manual PE parsing rather than static imports. The framework includes a method to locate the ntoskrnl base using IDT “scandown,” then dynamically resolves functions like `DbgPrint`. It emphasizes safe execution constraints (payload should run at IRQL 0 / PASSIVE_LEVEL) and suggests using work items or passive-level DPCs. It’s primarily useful for red teamers, exploit developers, and kernel security researchers building or testing ring-0 payloads, and it also includes defensive notes (HVCI/WDAC) relevant to blue teams. Testing guidance references Mandiant’s `flare-kscldr` loader and a kernel debugging VM setup.

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 kernel-mode PIC/shellcode in C++20
- Runtime dynamic linking of Native API / kernel exports (manual PE export resolution)
- Locating `ntoskrnl.exe` base via IDT scandown
- IRQL/PASSIVE_LEVEL constraints; work items and passive-level DPC execution
- Toolchain/build workflow (MSYS2, MinGW-w64, GNU Make, VS Code)

Use Cases:
- Develop and compile kernel-mode shellcode payloads for exploit chains or driver-assisted execution
- Prototype ring-0 post-exploitation actions while minimizing static import artifacts
- Test kernel shellcode in a VM using `flare-kscldr` and kernel debugging setups
- Research kernel export resolution and KASLR-related base discovery techniques
- Build reusable templates for kernel PIC coding standards (no globals, stack strings)

Keywords:
Windows kernel, ring 0, kernel-mode shellcode, PIC, position-independent code, C++20, x64, ntoskrnl, IDT scandown, KASLR, PE parsing, export resolution, runtime dynamic linking, DbgPrint, IRQL, PASSIVE_LEVEL, DPC, work item, HVCI, WDAC, MSYS2, MinGW-w64, GNU Make, flare-kscldr